# Editing Plans

This guide assumes that you have read the primer at <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions>. The primer shows the usual edit: read the snapshot, change the full document, and send it back. This guide covers the responses, conflicts, targeted edits, and plan settings.

## Replace the content

`PUT <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/content` with `content`, `base_revision`, and `change_summary`. Use this request for most edits: a rewrite, a new section, many small fixes, or a restructure. One request with all changes makes one clear version. Many small requests make many versions.

| Response | Meaning | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| `201` with `revision`, `version_id`, `applied` | CoPlan saved a new version. | Continue. |
| `200` with `"no_op": true` | The content did not change. | Nothing. |
| `409` with `current_revision` | Someone wrote after your read. | Read again, apply your change to the new content, and send it with the new revision. Do not send the same request with the old revision. |
| `409` with `"code": "human_edit_pending"` | A person edited by hand after your last read. | See [Hand edits](#hand-edits). |
| `409` with `"code": "edit_locked"` | Someone holds the edit lock, usually a person in the web editor. | See [Edit locks](#edit-locks). |
| `422` | `content` or `base_revision` is missing or not valid. | Fix the request. |

CoPlan compares your content with the current version and keeps comments anchored to text that you did not change. If your edit removes the text that a comment is anchored to, the thread becomes out of date. Address the comment before you delete its text.

Readers see small dots beside sections changed since their last visit. Dots clear after a short reading pause and return if that section receives another live edit. Hover or focus a dot to see who last updated that section and when. Readers can also open History or dismiss the markers. Extensive changes show one notice. Keep change explanations in `change_summary`, not in extra changelog sections.

New or renamed sections in live edits receive dots. Dismiss acknowledges the current updates; later edits can show fresh attention.

Presentation mode keeps the show stable. After it ends, readers see all queued updates.

## Hand edits

A person's hand edit has the highest authority in a plan. CoPlan merges agent edits that happen at the same time, but it does not merge over a hand edit. After a person edits a plan by hand, CoPlan refuses every agent write until that agent reads the plan again. Hand edits include edits in the web editor and checkbox selections.

The refusal includes the person's diff:

```json
{
  "error": "Blocked: Sam edited this plan by hand (an edit, now at v7) and you last read v5. ...",
  "code": "human_edit_pending",
  "current_revision": 7,
  "last_human_revision": 7,
  "last_seen_revision": 5,
  "human_edits": [
    {
      "revision": 7,
      "editor": "Sam",
      "edited_at": "2026-08-24T17:02:11Z",
      "change_summary": "Edited in web UI",
      "diff": "@@ -12,3 +12,3 @@\n-Ship behind a flag in Q4.\n+Ship behind a flag in Q3 — legal signed off on the earlier date.\n"
    }
  ]
}
```

1. Read the diff. It shows the words that the person chose.
2. Read the snapshot again: `GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/snapshot`. This read removes the block. A new `base_revision` alone does not.
3. Make your change on top of the current content. Do not send the content that you had before. It does not include the person's edit, so it deletes their words.
4. If your task conflicts with their edit, keep their text and explain the conflict in a comment.

This rule applies to every way that an agent writes: content replacement, operations, and edit-session commits (`POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/sessions/$SESSION_ID/commit`).

## Targeted edits: operations

To change one passage without sending the full document, send operations:

```bash
<%= @curl %> -X POST \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "base_revision": 7,
    "change_summary": "Switched the datastore to PostgreSQL",
    "operations": [
      {"op": "replace_exact", "old_text": "We use MySQL", "new_text": "We use PostgreSQL"}
    ]
  }' \
  "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/operations" | jq .
```

CoPlan applies all operations in one request as one version. If another agent wrote after your `base_revision`, CoPlan moves your operations onto the current content. This works for up to 20 revisions. If the target text changed, CoPlan returns `409`. Read the plan again and retry.

| `op` | Fields | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| `replace_exact` | `old_text`, `new_text`, optional `occurrence` (1-based) or `replace_all: true` | Replaces the text. Without `occurrence` or `replace_all`, the text must occur exactly once. |
| `insert_under_heading` | `heading` (the full line, such as `"## Testing"`), `content` | Inserts content after the heading. The heading must occur exactly once. |
| `delete_paragraph_containing` | `needle` | Deletes the one paragraph that contains the text. |
| `replace_section` | `heading`, `new_content`, optional `include_heading: false` | Replaces the heading and everything under it, up to the next heading of the same or a higher level. With `include_heading: false`, CoPlan keeps the heading line and replaces only the body. |

An operation that cannot apply returns `422` with the reason, and CoPlan saves nothing.

To collect operations from several requests into one version, open an edit session: `POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/sessions`. The response has `id` and `base_revision`. Send both, as `session_id` and `base_revision`, on each operation request; CoPlan holds the operations without saving them. Then `POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/sessions/$SESSION_ID/commit` with a `change_summary` saves them as one version. A session expires after 10 minutes. Most edits do not need one: a single operations request already saves as one version.

## Edit locks

A person who opens the web editor takes the plan's edit lock. While anyone else holds the lock, CoPlan refuses every write, including content replacement and operations, with `409` and `"code": "edit_locked"`. The lock expires 5 minutes after the holder stops. Wait, read the plan again, and retry. Do not retry in a tight loop.

You can take the lock yourself to keep a long series of edits together. Most edits do not need it.

- Take it: `POST <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/lease` with `{"lease_token": "<random string>"}`.
- Send the same `lease_token` with each content or operation request.
- Renew it before 5 minutes pass, and release it as soon as you finish. Both calls need the same `lease_token`. While you hold it, people cannot save in the editor.

```bash
<%= @curl %> -X PATCH -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "{\"lease_token\": \"$LEASE_TOKEN\"}" \
  "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/lease"     # renew
<%= @curl %> -X DELETE -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d "{\"lease_token\": \"$LEASE_TOKEN\"}" \
  "<%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/lease"     # release
```

## Plan settings

`PATCH <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID` changes the fields that you send and nothing else. Only the plan author can change these settings.

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| `title` | A string. The plan's URL follows the new title, and the old URL still works. |
| `plan_type` | A type name. CoPlan adds the new type's default tags. Restructure the content to match the new template. |
| `tags` | An array. It replaces the full tag list. |
| `folder_path` or `folder_id` | Moves the plan in its library. |
| `visibility` | `"published"` shares a private plan. |
| `archived` | `true` or `false`. |
| `references` | An array of `{url, key, title}`. |

See <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions/creating> for the rules on types, folders, tags, and visibility.

## History

`GET <%= @base %>/api/v1/plans/$PLAN_ID/versions` lists every version, newest first, with `revision`, `actor_type` (`human`, `local_agent`, and so on), `change_summary`, and `created_at`. Use it to find what changed and who changed it. Do not write a changelog in the plan.
